answer:
Big O notation (O) represents the asymptotic upper bound of an algorithm's running time. Formally, f(n) = O(g(n)) if there exist positive constants c and n0 such that 0 <= f(n) <= c*g(n) for all n >= n0.

Example: Linear Search
for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
  if(arr[i] == key) return i;
}
Worst-case Time Complexity = O(n) because in the worst case, every element must be inspected.

Role of Big O Notation:
Big O notation describes the limiting behavior of an algorithm as input size n grows to infinity, allowing objective comparisons independent of compiler, CPU speed, or OS.

Linear Search Algorithm:
1. Read array size n and target key.
2. Traverse array from index 0 to n - 1.
3. If arr[i] == key, return index i and terminate.
4. If loop completes without match, return -1 ("Element not found").

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class LinearSearch {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int[] arr = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
    int key = sc.nextInt();
    int pos = -1;
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      if(arr[i] == key) { pos = i; break; }
    }
    if(pos == -1) System.out.println("Element not found");
    else System.out.println("Element found at index " + pos);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Best Case: O(1) — target found at first index arr[0].
• Average Case: O(n) — target found around index n/2.
• Worst Case: O(n) — target is at last index or absent.
• Space Complexity: O(1) auxiliary space.

Asymptotic Notations:
• Big O (O): Upper bound (worst-case ceiling).
• Big Omega (Ω): Lower bound (best-case floor).
• Big Theta (Θ): Tight bound (simultaneous upper and lower asymptotic bound).
• Little o (o): Strict upper bound where growth rate is strictly smaller: lim_{n->inf} f(n)/g(n) = 0.

Single Traversal MinMax Algorithm:
1. Initialize min and max with arr[0].
2. For i from 1 to n - 1: update min if arr[i] < min, update max if arr[i] > max.
3. Print min and max.

Java Program:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class MinMax {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int[] arr = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
    int min = arr[0], max = arr[0];
    for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) {
      if(arr[i] < min) min = arr[i];
      if(arr[i] > max) max = arr[i];
    }
    System.out.println("Minimum = " + min);
    System.out.println("Maximum = " + max);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Best Case = Average Case = Worst Case = Θ(n) because every element from index 1 to n - 1 is compared.
• Space Complexity: O(1).

Importance of Performance Analysis:
1. Evaluates feasibility and runtime scaling prior to software production.
2. Prevents performance bottlenecks in high-throughput applications.
3. Allows comparison between competing algorithmic approaches.

CheckSorted Algorithm:
1. Traverse array from i = 0 to n - 2.
2. Compare adjacent pair arr[i] and arr[i+1].
3. If arr[i] > arr[i+1], array is unsorted; terminate immediately.
4. If loop completes, declare array sorted.

Java Program:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class CheckSorted {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int[] arr = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
    boolean sorted = true;
    for(int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
      if(arr[i] > arr[i+1]) { sorted = false; break; }
    }
    if(sorted) System.out.println("Array is Sorted");
    else System.out.println("Array is Not Sorted");
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Best Case: O(1) when the very first inversion is detected (arr[0] > arr[1]).
• Average & Worst Case: O(n) when array is sorted or inversion is at the tail.
• Space Complexity: O(1).

Recurrence Relations in Algorithm Analysis:
A recurrence relation expresses the running time of a recursive algorithm T(n) in terms of its subproblems for smaller input sizes.

Recursive Power Algorithm:
• Base Case: If n == 0, return 1.
• Recursive Step: Return x * power(x, n - 1).

Java Program:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class PowerRecursion {
  static long power(long x, int n) {
    if(n == 0) return 1;
    return x * power(x, n - 1);
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    long x = sc.nextLong();
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    System.out.println(power(x, n));
    sc.close();
  }
}

Recurrence Derivation:
• T(n) = T(n - 1) + 1 for n > 0
• T(0) = 1 (constant time for base case)
• Expanding: T(n) = T(n - 2) + 1 + 1 = ... = T(0) + n*1 = n + 1 = O(n).

Complexity Analysis:
• Time Complexity: O(n)
• Space Complexity: O(n) due to recursive call stack activation frames.

Divide and Conquer in Binary Search:
1. Divide: Calculate mid = (low + high) / 2.
2. Conquer: Compare key with arr[mid]. If key == arr[mid], match found. If key < arr[mid], search left half (high = mid - 1). If key > arr[mid], search right half (low = mid + 1).
3. Combine: Trivial (no combining required since single target is returned).

Java Program:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class BinarySearch {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int[] arr = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
    int key = sc.nextInt();
    int low = 0, high = n - 1;
    boolean found = false;
    while(low <= high) {
      int mid = (low + high) / 2;
      if(arr[mid] == key) {
        System.out.println("Element found at index " + mid);
        found = true; break;
      } else if(key < arr[mid]) high = mid - 1;
      else low = mid + 1;
    }
    if(!found) System.out.println("Element not found");
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Best Case: O(1) when key equals arr[mid] on first comparison.
• Average Case: O(log n)
• Worst Case: O(log n)
• Space Complexity: O(1) iterative.

Merge Sort Approach:
1. Divide: Split array at mid = (left + right) / 2 into two halves.
2. Conquer: Recursively sort left and right subarrays.
3. Combine: Merge the two sorted halves into a single sorted array using temporary buffer.

Java Program:
public class Mergesort2 {
  public static void conquer(int arr[], int left, int mid, int right) {
    int merge[] = new int[right - left + 1];
    int a = left, b = mid + 1, x = 0;
    while(a <= mid && b <= right) {
      if(arr[a] <= arr[b]) merge[x++] = arr[a++];
      else merge[x++] = arr[b++];
    }
    while(a <= mid) merge[x++] = arr[a++];
    while(b <= right) merge[x++] = arr[b++];
    for(int i = 0, j = left; i < merge.length; i++, j++) arr[j] = merge[i];
  }
  public static void divide(int arr[], int left, int right) {
    if(left >= right) return;
    int mid = (left + right) / 2;
    divide(arr, left, mid);
    divide(arr, mid + 1, right);
    conquer(arr, left, mid, right);
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    int arr[] = {5, 44, 3, 2, 1};
    divide(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    for(int v : arr) System.out.print(v + " ");
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Recurrence: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + O(n)
• Master Theorem Case 2: Time Complexity = O(n log n) in Best, Average, and Worst cases.
• Space Complexity: O(n) for auxiliary merge buffer.

Quick Sort Paradigm:
Developed by Tony Hoare (1959). Partitions the array around a pivot element such that all elements <= pivot are placed to its left and elements > pivot to its right.

Partition Algorithm:
1. Choose pivot = arr[lb].
2. Increment start pointer while arr[start] <= pivot.
3. Decrement end pointer while arr[end] > pivot.
4. If start < end, swap arr[start] and arr[end].
5. Finally, swap pivot arr[lb] with arr[end] and return end as pivot index.

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class quicksort1 {
  public static void quicksort(int arr[], int lb, int ub) {
    if(lb < ub) {
      int loc = partition(arr, lb, ub);
      quicksort(arr, lb, loc - 1);
      quicksort(arr, loc + 1, ub);
    }
  }
  static int partition(int arr[], int lb, int ub) {
    int pivot = arr[lb];
    int start = lb, end = ub;
    while(start < end) {
      while(start < ub && arr[start] <= pivot) start++;
      while(end > lb && arr[end] > pivot) end--;
      if(start < end) {
        int temp = arr[start]; arr[start] = arr[end]; arr[end] = temp;
      }
    }
    int temp = arr[lb]; arr[lb] = arr[end]; arr[end] = temp;
    return end;
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int arr[] = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
    quicksort(arr, 0, n - 1);
    for(int v : arr) System.out.print(v + " ");
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Best Case: O(n log n) when pivot always splits array in equal halves.
• Average Case: O(n log n)
• Worst Case: O(n^2) when array is already sorted or reverse sorted.
• Space Complexity: O(log n) call stack frames.

Job Sequencing with Deadlines:
Each job has (Job ID, Deadline, Profit) and takes 1 unit of time. The greedy strategy sorts jobs in descending order of profit and assigns each job to its latest available free slot before its deadline.

Java Program:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Jobsequence {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    String[] id = new String[n];
    int d[] = new int[n], p[] = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      id[i] = sc.next(); d[i] = sc.nextInt(); p[i] = sc.nextInt();
    }
    for(int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
      for(int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
        if(p[i] < p[j]) {
          int td = d[i]; d[i] = d[j]; d[j] = td;
          int tp = p[i]; p[i] = p[j]; p[j] = tp;
          String ts = id[i]; id[i] = id[j]; id[j] = ts;
        }
      }
    }
    boolean slot[] = new boolean[n];
    int profit = 0;
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for(int j = Math.min(d[i], n) - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
        if(!slot[j]) {
          slot[j] = true;
          System.out.print(id[i] + " ");
          profit += p[i];
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    System.out.println("\nProfit = " + profit);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Sorting jobs: O(n log n)
• Slot search and allocation: O(n * d) where d is maximum deadline.
• Worst-case Time Complexity: O(n^2) when d ≈ n.
• Space Complexity: O(n) for slot array.

Fractional Knapsack Problem:
Items can be broken into continuous fractions. The greedy choice property dictates selecting items with the highest value-to-weight ratio (v[i] / w[i]) first.

Java Program:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Knapsack {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    double w[] = new double[n], v[] = new double[n], r[] = new double[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      w[i] = sc.nextDouble(); v[i] = sc.nextDouble();
      r[i] = v[i] / w[i];
    }
    double cap = sc.nextDouble(), max = 0;
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for(int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
        if(r[i] < r[j]) {
          double tr = r[i]; r[i] = r[j]; r[j] = tr;
          double tw = w[i]; w[i] = w[j]; w[j] = tw;
          double tv = v[i]; v[i] = v[j]; v[j] = tv;
        }
      }
    }
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      if(w[i] <= cap) { max += v[i]; cap -= w[i]; }
      else { max += r[i] * cap; break; }
    }
    System.out.println(max);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Sorting items by ratio: O(n log n)
• Greedy selection pass: O(n)
• Total Time Complexity: O(n log n)
• Space Complexity: O(n).

0/1 Knapsack Problem (DP):
Unlike fractional knapsack, items cannot be broken. Each item must either be taken (1) or left behind (0). Overlapping subproblems are resolved using a 2D DP table.

Recurrence Relation:
• If weight[i-1] <= w:
  dp[i][w] = max(value[i-1] + dp[i-1][w - weight[i-1]], dp[i-1][w])
• Else:
  dp[i][w] = dp[i-1][w]

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class knadum {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int weight[] = new int[n], value[] = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) weight[i] = sc.nextInt();
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) value[i] = sc.nextInt();
    int cap = sc.nextInt();
    int dp[][] = new int[n + 1][cap + 1];
    for(int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for(int w = 1; w <= cap; w++) {
        if(weight[i - 1] <= w)
          dp[i][w] = Math.max(value[i - 1] + dp[i - 1][w - weight[i - 1]], dp[i - 1][w]);
        else dp[i][w] = dp[i - 1][w];
      }
    }
    System.out.println("Profit = " + dp[n][cap]);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity:
• Time Complexity: O(n * W) pseudo-polynomial
• Space Complexity: O(n * W) for DP table.

Floyd-Warshall Algorithm:
Computes the shortest path between all pairs of vertices in a directed weighted graph with positive or negative edge weights (no negative cycles) using dynamic programming.

State Equation:
dp[i][j] = min(dp[i][j], dp[i][k] + dp[k][j]) for intermediate pivot k from 0 to n - 1.

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Apsp {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int INF = 999;
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
      for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) dp[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
    for(int k = 0; k < n; k++)
      for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for(int j = 0; j < n; j++)
          if(dp[i][j] > dp[i][k] + dp[k][j])
            dp[i][j] = dp[i][k] + dp[k][j];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
      for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) System.out.print(dp[i][j] + " ");
      System.out.println();
    }
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Three nested loops from 0 to n - 1.
• Time Complexity: O(n^3)
• Space Complexity: O(n^2) for distance matrix.

Optimal Binary Search Tree (OBST):
Constructs a binary search tree from keys with given search frequencies such that the expected total search cost (depth * frequency) is minimized.

DP Recurrence:
For a subproblem interval [i, j], choose root r (i <= r <= j):
dp[i][j] = sum(freq[i..j]) + min_{r} (dp[i][r-1] + dp[r+1][j])

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Obst {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt();
    int freq[] = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) freq[i] = sc.nextInt();
    int dp[][] = new int[n][n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) dp[i][i] = freq[i];
    for(int len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
      for(int i = 0; i <= n - len; i++) {
        int j = i + len - 1;
        int sum = 0;
        for(int k = i; k <= j; k++) sum += freq[k];
        dp[i][j] = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for(int r = i; r <= j; r++) {
          int left = (r > i) ? dp[i][r - 1] : 0;
          int right = (r < j) ? dp[r + 1][j] : 0;
          int cost = left + sum + right;
          if(cost < dp[i][j]) dp[i][j] = cost;
        }
      }
    }
    System.out.println("Minimum cost is: " + dp[0][n - 1]);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity Analysis:
• Time Complexity: O(n^3) due to length, start position, and root search loops.
• Space Complexity: O(n^2).

Hamiltonian Cycle Problem:
A Hamiltonian Cycle in an undirected graph is a closed loop that visits every vertex exactly once and returns to the starting vertex.

Backtracking Approach:
1. Start at vertex 0 and build a path array.
2. In each step, test if candidate vertex v is safe: an edge must exist from path[pos-1] to v, and v must not be visited already.
3. Recursively attempt to complete the path.
4. When pos == n, check if an edge exists from path[n-1] to path[0]. If so, cycle found.
5. Backtrack by resetting path[pos] = -1 if path fails.

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class Main {
  static int[][] graph; static int[] path; static int n;
  static boolean isSafe(int v, int pos) {
    if(graph[path[pos - 1]][v] == 0) return false;
    for(int i = 0; i < pos; i++) if(path[i] == v) return false;
    return true;
  }
  static void hamiltonian(int pos) {
    if(pos == n) {
      if(graph[path[n - 1]][path[0]] == 1) {
        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) System.out.print(path[i] + " ");
        System.out.println(path[0]);
      }
      return;
    }
    for(int v = 1; v < n; v++) {
      if(isSafe(v, pos)) {
        path[pos] = v;
        hamiltonian(pos + 1);
        path[pos] = -1;
      }
    }
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    n = sc.nextInt();
    graph = new int[n][n]; path = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
      for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) graph[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
    path[0] = sc.nextInt();
    for(int i = 1; i < n; i++) path[i] = -1;
    hamiltonian(1);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity: O(N!).

Graph Coloring Problem:
Assign one of m colors to each vertex in graph G such that no two adjacent vertices share the same color.

Backtracking Method:
1. Try coloring vertex v with color c = 1..m.
2. Verify isSafe(v, c): no adjacent vertex graph[v][i] == 1 already possesses color c.
3. If safe, assign color[v] = c and recursively color vertex v + 1.
4. If no color leads to a solution, backtrack: color[v] = 0.

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
class GraphColoring {
  static int[][] graph; static int[] color; static int m, n;
  static boolean isSafe(int v, int c) {
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
      if(graph[v][i] == 1 && color[i] == c) return false;
    return true;
  }
  static void graphColor(int v) {
    if(v == n) {
      for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) System.out.print(color[i] + " ");
      System.out.println(); return;
    }
    for(int c = 1; c <= m; c++) {
      if(isSafe(v, c)) {
        color[v] = c;
        graphColor(v + 1);
        color[v] = 0;
      }
    }
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    n = sc.nextInt(); graph = new int[n][n]; color = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++)
      for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) graph[i][j] = sc.nextInt();
    m = sc.nextInt();
    graphColor(0);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity: O(m^V).

N-Queens Problem:
Place N chess queens on an N x N chessboard such that no two queens attack each other (no two queens in the same row, column, or diagonal).

Backtracking Strategy:
1. Place queens row by row.
2. For queen at row, test each col = 0..n-1 using isSafe:
   - Check column for existing queen.
   - Check upper-left diagonal (row - 1, col - 1).
   - Check upper-right diagonal (row - 1, col + 1).
3. If safe, set board[row][col] = 1 and recurse to row + 1.
4. If no column in subsequent rows succeeds, backtrack: set board[row][col] = 0.

Java Implementation:
import java.util.Scanner;
public class NQueens {
  static int n; static int[][] board;
  static boolean isSafe(int row, int col) {
    for(int i = 0; i < row; i++) if(board[i][col] == 1) return false;
    for(int i = row - 1, j = col - 1; i >= 0 && j >= 0; i--, j--)
      if(board[i][j] == 1) return false;
    for(int i = row - 1, j = col + 1; i >= 0 && j < n; i--, j++)
      if(board[i][j] == 1) return false;
    return true;
  }
  static boolean solve(int row) {
    if(row == n) return true;
    for(int col = 0; col < n; col++) {
      if(isSafe(row, col)) {
        board[row][col] = 1;
        if(solve(row + 1)) return true;
        board[row][col] = 0;
      }
    }
    return false;
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    n = sc.nextInt(); board = new int[n][n];
    if(solve(0)) {
      for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) System.out.print(board[i][j] + " ");
        System.out.println();
      }
    } else System.out.println("No solution");
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity: O(N!).

Sum of Subsets Problem:
Find all subsets of a given set of n positive integers whose elements sum up to a specified target number.

Backtracking Approach:
1. Sort array (optional, for pruning).
2. If current sum == target, print current subset.
3. If index == n or sum > target, prune search tree.
4. Include element: subset.add(arr[index]), recurse with sum + arr[index].
5. Backtrack: remove last element and recurse without including arr[index].

Java Implementation:
import java.util.*;
public class SumOfSubset {
  static void sumsubsets(int arr[], int index, int sum, int target, ArrayList<Integer> subset) {
    if(sum == target) {
      System.out.println(subset); return;
    }
    if(index == arr.length || sum > target) return;
    subset.add(arr[index]);
    sumsubsets(arr, index + 1, sum + arr[index], target, subset);
    subset.remove(subset.size() - 1);
    sumsubsets(arr, index + 1, sum, target, subset);
  }
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
    int n = sc.nextInt(); int[] arr = new int[n];
    for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] = sc.nextInt();
    int target = sc.nextInt();
    ArrayList<Integer> subset = new ArrayList<>();
    System.out.println("Subsets with sum " + target + ":");
    sumsubsets(arr, 0, 0, target, subset);
    sc.close();
  }
}

Complexity: O(2^n).
